WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.048
Hi! Welcome to your first German lesson.

2
00:00:06.148 --> 00:00:12.324
Today you'll learn to introduce yourself: who you are and where you're from… in German.

3
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:22.501
Let's start with greetings. In German the greeting changes with the time of day. In the morning we say:

4
00:00:22.601 --> 00:00:24.769
Good morning!

5
00:00:25.169 --> 00:00:28.177
During the day we say:

6
00:00:28.277 --> 00:00:30.445
Good day!

7
00:00:30.845 --> 00:00:34.645
And in the evening, when you arrive somewhere:

8
00:00:34.745 --> 00:00:37.009
Good evening!

9
00:00:37.409 --> 00:00:41.881
Careful: this last one is only said before going to bed.

10
00:00:41.981 --> 00:00:44.077
Good night!

11
00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:50.671
And there's a wildcard that works at any time, especially with friends:

12
00:00:50.771 --> 00:00:52.843
Hi!

13
00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:59.365
Now, your name. Look at this sentence:

14
00:00:59.465 --> 00:01:01.873
My name is Lena.

15
00:01:02.273 --> 00:01:10.561
“Ich” means “I”, and “heiße” comes from heißen: to be called. The letter ß sounds like a double s.

16
00:01:10.661 --> 00:01:14.221
You can also simply say:

17
00:01:14.321 --> 00:01:16.465
I'm Lena.

18
00:01:16.865 --> 00:01:21.217
“Ich bin” is “I am”. That easy!

19
00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:27.297
And where are you from? For that we use “komme aus”:

20
00:01:27.397 --> 00:01:30.165
I'm from Colombia.

21
00:01:30.565 --> 00:01:35.493
Look at the verb kommen, to come: only the ending changes.

22
00:01:35.593 --> 00:01:39.297
With “ich”, it ends in e: komme.

23
00:01:39.397 --> 00:01:43.173
With “du”, it ends in s-t: kommst.

24
00:01:43.273 --> 00:01:47.433
And with “Sie”, it's the same as the infinitive: kommen.

25
00:01:48.833 --> 00:01:56.473
A very German detail: there are two ways to say “you”. With friends, family and children you use “du”.

26
00:01:56.573 --> 00:01:58.645
What's your name?

27
00:01:59.045 --> 00:02:07.021
With a teacher, in a shop or with adults you don't know, you use “Sie”, capitalised: the formal “you”.

28
00:02:07.121 --> 00:02:09.385
What's your name? (formal)

29
00:02:09.785 --> 00:02:14.569
If in doubt, use “Sie”. It's more polite!

30
00:02:15.967 --> 00:02:20.319
Now listen to a whole conversation between Lena and Mateo.

31
00:02:20.419 --> 00:02:25.899
Hi! I'm Lena. What's your name?

32
00:02:26.299 --> 00:02:28.683
My name is Mateo.

33
00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:31.251
Where are you from?

34
00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:35.883
I'm from Colombia. And you?

35
00:02:36.283 --> 00:02:40.467
I'm from Austria. Bye!

36
00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:48.655
Now you! Say your name and where you're from out loud. I'll wait.

37
00:02:55.067 --> 00:02:58.531
Very good! Let's review.

38
00:02:58.631 --> 00:03:07.327
You can greet, say your name and where you're from, and know when to use du or Sie. Now practise in the exercises!

39
00:03:07.427 --> 00:03:09.499
See you soon!
